WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:06.990
I'm an independent IT consultant, and when it comes to taxes,
I really just want to know two things.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:11.270
What do I need to file next?
And when do I need to file it?

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:19.750
The problem is tax websites show me dozens of forms, rules,
thresholds, and deadlines, most of which I don't fully understand.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:26.830
And when I search for tutorials, I usually end up with even more
information and somehow even more confusion.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:30.710
So I built this. This is My Next Filing.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:37.110
It's a small tool designed to answer one question:
What do I need to do next?

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:43.590
Let me show you how it works. I'll use an example here,
and just to be clear, all of these amounts are fictional.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:48.070
The app supports one clearly defined type of user.
That's intentional.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:54.950
By keeping the scope narrow, the app can avoid giving answers
for situations it isn't designed to handle.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:02.510
In my example, I enter my professional receipts, my bank interest,
my available tax credits, and my GST turnover.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.350
Each field explains what the number means
and what I'm supposed to enter.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:13.230
And if I make a mistake or want to change something,
I can simply go back and edit my answers.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:18.150
Once everything looks right, I calculate my plan.
And this is the result.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:24.590
The app shows me my next tax date, the estimated amount involved,
and my current GST threshold status.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:31.830
Below that, I get the complete calendar in date order,
so I can see what is coming after that as well.

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:40.750
And if I want to understand where a number came from, I can open
the calculation, see how it was worked out, and check the official sources.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:47.110
The app does not file a return. It does not make payments,
and it does not try to replace a tax professional.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:52.710
I just wanted one page that tells me what matters right now.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:58.270
I used Codex throughout the build.
It helped me turn the initial idea into this working web app.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:03.350
And it also helped me test and check
the important user paths along the way.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:07.270
The app itself is intentionally simple from a privacy perspective.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:15.070
There's no account, no database, and no third-party API connection.
All of the calculations run directly in the browser.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.830
And when the page is closed, the answers disappear.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:22.830
There is another problem I wanted to account for:
tax rules change.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:29.350
So the rules used by the app are stored locally,
linked back to official sources, and given an expiry date.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:39.550
If a rule becomes too old or is no longer considered valid,
the calculation stops. It would rather give me no answer
than confidently give me an outdated one.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:45.070
And that's really the idea behind My Next Filing.
It's a small and deliberately limited tool.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:50.270
It doesn't try to understand every tax situation.
It doesn't try to do everything for me.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:56.550
It just tries to answer one useful question:
What do I need to do next?

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.910
And of course, it provides general guidance, not tax advice.
